  <ferc:DisclosureOfEstimatedAnnualEffectAndNatureOfAnyImportantWageScaleChangesTextBlock id="f-25" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;8. None&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfEstimatedAnnualEffectAndNatureOfAnyImportantWageScaleChangesTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfStatusOfAnyMateriallyImportantLegalProceedingsTextBlock id="f-26" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;9. For information, see the December 31, 2025 Notes to Financial Statements - Note 3 - Regulatory Matters.&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfStatusOfAnyMateriallyImportantLegalProceedingsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfMateriallyImportantTransactionsOfTheRespondentNotDisclosedElsewhereTextBlock id="f-27" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;10. None&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfMateriallyImportantTransactionsOfTheRespondentNotDisclosedElsewhereTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantChangesRelatedToRespondentCompanyAppearingInAnnualReportToStockholdersTextBlock id="f-28" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;12. None&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfImportantChangesRelatedToRespondentCompanyAppearingInAnnualReportToStockholdersTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock id="f-29" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;13. Changes in officers during the period:&lt;br/&gt;05/22/2025 - Michael H. Dunne resigned as Vice President&lt;br/&gt;08/05/2025 - Matthew C. Coates was appointed as Vice President&lt;br/&gt;10/13/2025 - Matt Pawlowski was appointed as Vice President&lt;br/&gt;10/22/2025 - Eduardo Devarona was appointed as Vice President&lt;br/&gt;10/22/2025 - Elizaneth Solano resigned as Vice President&lt;br/&gt;11/20/2025 - Luigi Grisolia was appointed President&lt;br/&gt;11/20/2025 - Natalie F. Smith resigned as President&lt;br/&gt;11/20/2025 - Eduardo Devarona resigned as Vice President&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfChangesInOfficersDirectorsMajorSecurityHoldersAndVotingPowersOfTheRespondentTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock id="f-30" contextRef="c-1">&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;14. None&lt;/span&gt;</ferc:DisclosureOfSignificantEventThatRespondentParticipatesInACashManagementProgramsTextBlock>
  <ferc:DisclosureOfImportantDisclosuresRegardingTheFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-31" contextRef="c-1">&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;GRIDLIANCE HEARTLAND LLC&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTES TO FINANCIAL STATEMENTS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;FOR THE YEARS ENDED DECEMBER 31, 2025 AND 2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;(Dollars in thousands, unless otherwise noted) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;Introduction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The accompanying financial statements were prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission ("FERC") as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases, which is a comprehensive basis of accounting that varies from generally accepted accounting principles in the United States of America ("GAAP"). These requirements differ from GAAP related to (1) the presentation of regulatory debits and credits (2) the presentation of deferred income taxes (3) the presentation of regulatory assets and liabilities (4) presentation of asset removal costs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; Accordingly, certain footnotes are not prepared on the same basis as the financial statements presented herein. The information below is presented in millions unless otherwise stated. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:12pt;margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 1 - ORGANIZATION AND BUSINESS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;GridLiance Heartland LLC (&#8220;the Company&#8221;) was formed in 2014, is an indirect subsidiary of GridLiance Holdco, LLC (&#8220;GL&#8221;, &#8220;GridLiance LLC&#8221;), a Delaware limited partnership, which in turn is a wholly owned subsidiary of NextEra Energy Transmission, LLC ("NEET"). NEET is an indirect subsidiary of NextEra Energy, Inc., a company listed on the New York Stock Exchange. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company, a single member limited liability company organized under the laws of Delaware, is a rate regulated transmission provider in Illinois and Kentucky. Its purpose is to collaborate with rural electric cooperatives, municipal utilities, joint action agencies and others to plan for the future of the grid within Mid-continent Independent System Operator, Inc. (&#8220;MISO&#8221;) located in the United States. The Company invests in necessary electric infrastructure and implements strategies to improve system reliability and resiliency and reduce overall costs to customers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:12pt;margin-top:12pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 2 - SIGNIFICANT ACCOUNTING POLICIES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt; - The preparation of financial statements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities and disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities at the date of the financial statements and the reported amounts of revenues and expenses during the reporting period. Actual results could differ from those estimates.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Accounting - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company is subject to rate regulation by the FERC. The Company&#8217;s rates are designed to recover the cost of providing electric service to its customers and currently include a return on invested capital. Revenues from the Company's cost based formula rates are recorded when earned. The Company achieves matching of costs and related revenues by deferring the net under recovery or over recovery. Any under recovered costs or over recovered revenues are collected from or returned to customers in subsequent periods.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As a regulated utility, the Company records assets and liabilities that result from the regulated ratemaking process that would otherwise not be recorded under GAAP for non-regulated companies. Regulatory assets represent costs that will be included as a component of future tariff rates and regulatory liabilities represent amounts that have been collected in current rates to recover costs that are expected to be incurred, or refunded to customers, in future periods.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;When regulatory assets are probable of recovery through regulated rates, the Company records them as assets on the balance sheet. Once a regulatory asset is recorded, the Company reviews the probability of recovery at each balance sheet date, considering the impact of any changes or events during the period. If the likelihood of future recovery of any regulatory asset becomes less than probable, the Company will write off that regulatory asset as a charge against income.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 3 &#8211; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Matter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;s for additional information. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents - All highly liquid investments with a maturity at the date of purchase of three months or less are considered cash. Cash for short-term working capital needs is held in financial institutions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Revenue Recognition - Operating revenues are derived primarily from transmission services. Revenues are determined using approved tariff rates and are recognized as transmission service is provided. The tariff rates permit the Company to recover allowable costs and earn a return on investments in property, plant and equipment. The obligation to provide transmission services is satisfied over time as the customer simultaneously receives and consumes benefits. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;See Note 3 &#8211; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Regulatory Matter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;s for additional information. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Accounts Receivable &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;- Receivables are associated with revenues earned from contracts with customers consisting of both billed and unbilled amounts. Receivables represent unconditional rights to consideration and reflect the differences in timing of revenue recognition and cash collections. If any of the customer receivable balances should be deemed uncollectible, it could have a material adverse effect on the Company's results of operations and financial condition. However, management does not believe significant credit risk existed at December 31, 2025 and 2024. Accounts receivable balances would be adjusted for any write-offs and any estimated allowance for doubtful accounts, which is calculated on the basis of the expected credit losses. The allowance for doubtful accounts is reviewed periodically, considering historical and current information such as average write-offs to estimate the expected credit losses. There was no allowance for doubtful accounts recorded as of December 31, 2025 or 2024, respectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment, Net - Property, plant and equipment, net consists primarily of transmission assets. Property, plant and equipment are recorded at cost and depreciated on a straight-line average remaining life basis. Accumulated depreciation consists of the cost of units of property retired less estimated net salvage value. Maintenance and repairs of property as well as replacements and renewals of items determined to be less than units of property are charged to operations and maintenance expenses. &lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Construction work in progress includes construction materials, progress payments on major equipment contracts, third-party engineering costs and other costs directly associated with the construction of various projects. Upon completion of the projects, these costs are transferred to property, plant and equipment in service.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Depreciation of the Company's transmission assets is provided on a straight-line basis, primarily over its average remaining useful life as approved by the FERC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;See Note 4 - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Utility Plant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt; for additional information. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:justify"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt;Impairment of Long-Lived Assets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:115%"&gt; - Long-lived assets that are held and used are reviewed for impairment whenever events or changes in circumstances indicate carrying values may not be recoverable. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company concluded no impairment adjustments were necessary.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;AFUDC - Allowance for funds used during construction ("AFUDC") represents the estimated costs of borrowed and equity funds used to finance regulated plant additions before they go into service and is capitalized as part of the cost of construction. AFUDC is recoverable through rates over the life of the related asset once that asset is placed in service. AFUDC related to the cost of borrowed funds is recorded as a reduction to interest expense. AFUDC related to the cost of equity funds is recorded as a component of other income. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;As a component of construction work in progress, AFUDC will be placed into service along with other construction costs attributable to assets placed in service and depreciated over the useful lives of those assets in accordance with rates approved by FERC.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Income Taxes - Deferred income taxes are provided on all significant temporary differences between the financial statement and tax basis of assets or liabilities. The significant temporary differences result primarily from property basis differences caused by differences in book and tax depreciation.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Included in regulatory assets on the Company's balance sheets is the revenue equivalent of the difference in accumulated deferred income taxes computed under accounting rules, as compared to regulatory accounting rules. The resulting excess deferred taxes will be returned to rate payers in the future. The Company is a member of a consolidated group, NextEra Energy and subsidiaries, which files a consolidated tax return. The income tax accounts in the accompanying financial statements are determined as if the Company filed a separate tax return. &lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="margin-bottom:8pt"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:700;line-height:120%"&gt;NOTE 3 - REGULATORY MATTERS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Formula Rate Filings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Company principally generates revenue through transmitting electricity to its customers through MISO and the Open Access Transmission Tariff ("OATT") (non-MISO). Substantially all of the Company&#8217;s material revenue streams consist of Commission-approved, tariff-based revenue. The projected revenue requirement for the transmission of electricity by the Company is calculated prior to the beginning of each year (the &#8220;Rate Year&#8221;) using the FERC-approved formula rate template and billed to and collected from the customers ratably over the Rate Year. The Company recognizes revenue proportionally throughout the year based on these approved cost-based formula rates.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 29, 2019, the Commission accepted, subject to condition, the Company&#8217;s Formula Rate to determine and recover the costs of the Company&#8217;s investment in transmission facilities located in the MISO region. The Commission also approved the Company&#8217;s rate of return on equity ("ROE") of 10.82% including a 50-basis points ROE adder in recognition of the Company joining MISO, a regional transmission organization ("RTO") as transmission owner ("RTO Participation Adder"), subject to the outcome of pending proceedings. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 31, 2020, the Commission approved the implementation of the Company&#8217;s annual transmission revenue requirement ("ATRR") under its Formula Rate, effective upon placing the Company&#8217;s assets under MISO&#8217;s control, which occurred on March 1, 2020. The ATRR reflects an ROE of 10.38% (9.88% with a 50 basis point adder) and the Commission also approved tariff changes to implement the Rate Mitigation Credits proposed by the Company. The Rate Mitigation Credits provide for a fixed credit of $2.65 million on an annualized basis to offset the rate impact of including the first four lines in the MISO tariff, with such credit to be given for five years from closing of the Proposed Transaction (the Rate Mitigation Period). The Company&#8217;s Formula Rate also credits an additional fixed amount of $0.95 million to offset the rate impact of the remaining two lines for five years following the date they were added to the MISO tariff on August 31, 2022.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On February 21, 2020, to address the conditions on its Formula Rates imposed by the Commission&#8217;s order approving the acquisition of the EEI assets, the Company submitted a compliance filing providing for a continuation of the Rate Mitigation Credits for the two lines not in MISO for five years from the time they are added to the MISO tariff and providing that the Company may not recover its Regulatory Asset through rates during the initial five years of the Rate Mitigation Period.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On May 21, 2020, the Commission issued an order on rehearing refining its methodology and announcing a new policy for analyzing the base ROE component of public utility rates, which changed the MISO transmission owners' base ROE from 9.88% to 10.02%, which raised the Company&#8217;s ROE to 10.52%, inclusive of a 50-basis point ROE adder. In 2022, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;FERC's orders were vacated by the U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia and remanded back to the Commission for further consideration. On October 17, 2024, the Commission issued an Order on Remand whereby the MISO transmission owners' base ROE was set to 9.98%, resulting in a 10.48% ROE for the Company, inclusive of a 50-basis point ROE adder.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;For the two transmission lines that were not in the MISO region, on January 29, 2019, the Commission approved the Company&#8217;s Open Access Transmission Tariff ("OATT") and Formula Rate, subject to conditions, refund, hearing and settlement judge procedures to establish its ROE. Following settlement discussions with the parties, on May 28, 2020, the Company filed an Offer of Settlement for a 9.8% ROE, which was approved via Commission Letter Order on September 28, 2020.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On September 30, 2020, MISO submitted a filing and revised tariff sheets on behalf of the Company in compliance with Order No. 864, Public Utility Transmission Rate Changes to Address Accumulated Deferred Income Taxes.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On September 28, 2020, the Company made the same filing for the two transmission lines that were not in MISO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="background-color:#ffffff;color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;, which is still pending Commission approval.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On April 1, 2021, the Company submitted tariff revisions to incorporate a new income tax allowance calculation for its assets that are not in MISO, consistent with a change in the Company&#8217;s upstream ownership.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 31, 2021, the Company became a wholly owned, indirect subsidiary of NextEra Energy, Inc., a C corporation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Also on April 1, 2021, MISO submitted similar revisions on the Company&#8217;s behalf for the income tax allowance component of its revenue requirement for assets under MISO&#8217;s functional control.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The Commission accepted both filings on May 20, 2021 via Letter Orders.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In November 2024, the Company agreed to acquire transmission assets from Logansport Utilities for $5.0 million, contingent on receiving various state and federal regulatory approvals. The Company closed on the transaction on February 1, 2026, upon receipt of FERC approval for revisions to the MISO Tariff to include GridLiance Heartland in the Duke Energy Indiana, LLC pricing zone (FERC Docket No. ER25-2766).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;In 2025, the Company entered into a Joint Development Agreement with Dairyland Power Cooperative to jointly develop, construct, and own a portion of the North Rochester &#8211; Columbia 765 kV transmission line that was approved as part of MISO's LRTP Tranche 2.1 portfolio (known as the &#8220;MariBell Project&#8221;). The Company&#8217;s ownership interest in the Maribell Project is 40.0 percent. On May 30, 2025 as amended on June 6, 2025, the Company filed in FERC Docket No. ER25-2391 a request for CWIP and Abandoned Plant incentives associated with the project. The Commission accepted the application on August 5, 2025.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Once completed, the Company will be entitled to a proportionate share of the output from the MariBell Project. It is also responsible for its share of the operating costs, as well as providing its own financing. Accordingly, when operational, the Company's proportionate share of the facilities and related revenues and expenses will be included in the appropriate balance sheet and statement of income. As of December 31, 2025, $2,992 is included within Construction work in progress &#8211; Electric related to the MariBell project.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:103%"&gt;State Regulatory Proceedings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 9, 2020, the Company filed a petition for declaratory ruling with the Illinois Commerce Commission ("ICC") seeking confirmation that the Company is not a public utility under Illinois law. If the ICC determines that the Company is a public utility, the Company requested that the Commission identify the applicability of certain statutes governing the standard of review that would apply in seeking a Certificate of Public Convenience and Necessity ("CPCN") to operate in the state. On March 26, 2020, the ICC Administrative Law Judge ("ALJ") requested additional briefing on whether a declaratory ruling is the appropriate proceeding for determining whether the Company is a public utility. On June 6, 2020, the ICC issued an order denying the Company&#8217;s petition for declaratory ruling, stating that it would be more expedient to make that determination in the Ameren Illinois Company ("Ameren") complaint proceeding discussed below. On July 15, 2020, the Commission denied the Company&#8217;s request for rehearing. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On March 9, 2020, Ameren filed a complaint against the Company at the ICC, requesting that the ICC either find that the Company is in violation of the Illinois Public Utilities Act and, thus not lawfully operating as a public utility in Illinois or investigate whether the Company can operate as a public utility. A hearing was held in March 2022. On September 26, 2022, the ALJ issued a proposed interim order finding that the ICC does have subject matter jurisdiction and that &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;GridLiance Heartland is a public utility and required to file a CPCN application. The ICC voted to approve the ALJ&#8217;s proposed interim order on November 17, 2022, requiring the Company to file a CPCN application by January 17, 2023. On December 27, 2023, the ALJ ruled that the Company was a public utility and the ICC was just in their order to require a CPCN. The Company appealed this interim order to the Illinois Appellate Court for the Fifth District, which appeal was denied on December 26, 2023. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On January 17, 2023, the Company filed its CPCN application.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;Ameren has filed leave to intervene and was granted party status.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; O&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;n Oct. 12, 2023, the Commission ruled that the Company&#8217;s CPCN should include information regarding whether its ownership of the facilities is the least-cost means of serving the need for the facilities.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:12pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;The ALJ directed the Company to supplement its CPCN application by March 1, 2024, which supplement was timely filed. Ameren and ICC Staff filed testimony on June 17, 2024, the Company filed rebuttal testimony on July 22, 2024, and Ameren and ICC Staff filed rebuttal testimony on August 26, 2024. An evidentiary hearing was scheduled for October 29-31, 2024. In late October, the parties notified the ALJ that they were engaged in settlement discussions and requested abatement of the hearing to allow the settlement to be finalized. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:120%"&gt;On December 10, 2024, the Company and Ameren filed a settlement that would withdraw Ameren's objections to the grant the CPCN and to resolve the Complaint. ICC Staff does not oppose the settlement. The ICC approved the settlement on January 16, 2025, granting GridLiance Heartland's CPCN. Per the terms of the settlement, Ameren filed with withdrew its complaint against the Company on February 24, 2025, which the ICC granted on March 13, 2025, resolving the complaint proceeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-style:italic;font-weight:700;line-height:115%"&gt;Regulatory Assets and Liabilities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:10pt;font-weight:400;line-height:107%"&gt;The Company&#8217;s regulatory asset and liability balances are shown below as of December 31:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style="text-align:center"&gt;&lt;table style="border-collapse:collapse;display:inline-table;margin-bottom:5pt;vertical-align:text-bottom;width:99.861%"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:69.136%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.356%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:13.225%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.356%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:1.0%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:13.227%"/&gt;&lt;td style="width:0.1%"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="padding:2px 1pt;text-align:center;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Regulatory Assets:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;border-top:1pt solid #000000;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 7pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;Pre-commercial costs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;38,423&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;38,423&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 7pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2025 cost under recovery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;1,304&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;&#8212;&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#cceeff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 7pt;text-align:left;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;2024 cost under recovery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-family:'Arial',sans-serif;font-size:9pt;font-weight:400;line-height:100%"&gt;703&#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 1pt 2px 0;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="3" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:0 1pt"/&gt;&lt;td colspan="2" style="background-color:#ffffff;padding:2px 0 2px 1pt;text-align:right;vertical-align:bottom"&gt;&lt;span style="color:#000000;font-